.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-SEAL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-seal
.Nd remove a disk image's machine identity so it can be cloned
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar disk
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
opens the system on
.Ar disk ,
a stopped machine's disk or an image file, and removes what makes it one
particular machine, so that every machine made from it starts as a new
one:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
.Pa /etc/machine-id
is emptied and
.Pa /var/lib/dbus/machine-id
removed; systemd makes a new one at the next boot;
.It
the ssh host keys are removed; they are made again at the next boot;
.It
.Pa /etc/hostid
is removed;
.It
the WireGuard mesh keys, configurations and node id, and the units that
start them, are removed; enrolling the machine makes new ones;
.It
DHCP leases are forgotten;
.It
cloud-init's state is cleared, and a list of every datasource it knows is
written, so the image finds its settings on any platform;
.It
the journal, the login records and root's and every user's shell history
are cleared;
.It
.Pa /etc/kldload/seal.info
records when, where and from what it was sealed.
.El
.Pp
Two shapes of disk are understood: a partition table whose largest
partition holds the root filesystem (ext4, XFS, or Btrfs with the system
in subvolumes, as cloud images are), and ZFS on root, as kldload installs,
whose pool is imported under another name if this host has one of the same
name, and whose boot environment is mounted and sealed.
.Pp
It refuses a disk that is in use: mounted, or any partition of it; swap;
a device of a pool imported on this host; attached to a running or paused
virtual machine; or, for a file, already attached to a loop device.
Each is checked on the device the path resolves to, so an alias such as
.Pa /dev/zd192
for
.Pa /dev/zvol/rpool/vms/web01
is caught as well.
.Pp
Sealing writes to
.Ar disk .
To seal a copy and leave the original as it is, use
.Xr kexport 8 ,
which does that by default.
.Xr kvm-golden 8
seals the golden it builds.
.Pp
It must run as root.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ar disk
A block device \(em a ZFS volume, a disk, a partition \(em or a raw image
file.
A file is attached to a loop device for the duration.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the usage and exit 0.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /etc/kldload/seal.info
Written into the sealed system.
.It Pa /etc/cloud/cloud.cfg.d/99-kldload-seal.cfg
The datasource list, written when the system has cloud-init.
.It Pa /var/tmp/kldload-seal.XXXXXX
Where the disk is mounted while it is sealed; removed after.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
Sealed.
.It 1
The disk is in use or is not a disk, no root filesystem was found, or a
step failed.
.It 2
No disk given.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Printed on a kldload host on 2026-09-29, against throwaway machines cloned
from a Rocky Linux golden, a pool made on a loop device, and a file
attached to another; all were removed afterwards.
.Ss Sealing a stopped machine's disk
Before, the machine had a 32-character machine-id and six ssh host key
files:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-seal /dev/zvol/rpool/vms/sl-3359442-b
[seal] detected shape: fs on /dev/zvol/rpool/vms/sl-3359442-b
[seal] resolved /dev/zvol/rpool/vms/sl-3359442-b → /dev/zd192
[seal] mounting root partition /dev/zd192p4 (39856 MB) at /var/tmp/kldload-seal.vpHwOe
[seal] sealing /var/tmp/kldload-seal.vpHwOe
[seal]   cleared /etc/machine-id
[seal]   removed /etc/ssh/ssh_host_* keys
[seal]   removed /etc/hostid
[seal]   removed the WireGuard mesh identity (keys, configs, node id)
[seal]   forgot DHCP leases
[seal]   cloud-init: cleared seed + wrote portable datasource_list
[seal]   cleared journald + wtmp/btmp/lastlog
[seal]   stamped /etc/kldload/seal.info
[seal] seal complete
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Pp
After, from the disk mounted read-only:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# mount -o ro,norecovery /dev/zd192p4 /var/tmp/sealtest-3359442/mnt
# stat -c %s /var/tmp/sealtest-3359442/mnt/etc/machine-id
0
# ls /var/tmp/sealtest-3359442/mnt/etc/ssh/ | grep -c host
0
# cat /var/tmp/sealtest-3359442/mnt/etc/kldload/seal.info
sealed: 2026-09-29T23:34:14-07:00
sealed-by: kldload-seal 20260929
sealed-on: onyx
source: /dev/zvol/rpool/vms/sl-3359442-b
.Ed
.Ss What it refuses
The same machine's disk, by its other name, with its root partition
mounted on the host, then with the machine running; a pool's device; a
file on a loop device:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-seal /dev/zd192
[seal][error] /dev/zd192 (/dev/zd192) or a partition of it is mounted or in use as swap on this host — refusing
# (exited 1)
# kldload-seal /dev/zd192
[seal][error] /dev/zd192 is attached to the running libvirt domain sl-3359442 — shut it down first
# (exited 1)
# kldload-seal /dev/loop0
[seal][error] /dev/loop0 (/dev/loop0) is a device of a pool imported on this host — refusing
# (exited 1)
# kldload-seal /var/tmp/sealtest-3359442/busy.img
[seal][error] /var/tmp/sealtest-3359442/busy.img is attached to a loop device (/dev/loop1 ) — in use, refusing
# (exited 1)
# kldload-seal /dev/nosuch
[seal][error] /dev/nosuch is not a block device or file
# (exited 1)
# kldload-seal
kldload-seal — sysprep a disk image for portable / golden deployment
\&...
# (exited 2)
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "[seal][error] DISK (DEVICE) or a partition of it is mounted or in use as swap on this host \(em refusing"
.It "[seal][error] DISK (DEVICE) is a device of a pool imported on this host \(em refusing"
.It "[seal][error] DISK is attached to the running libvirt domain VM \(em shut it down first"
.It "[seal][error] DISK is attached to a loop device (LOOP) \(em in use, refusing"
Nothing was changed.
.It "[seal][error] no partitions found on DEVICE ..."
The disk has no partition table; a bare filesystem image cannot be
sealed.
.It "[seal][error] DEVICE mounted but no /etc found \(em wrong partition?"
The largest partition is not the root filesystem.
.It "[seal][error] pool 'POOL' has no bootfs property \(em cannot find root dataset"
A ZFS disk whose pool names no boot environment.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr systemd-machine-id-setup 1 ,
.Xr kexport 8 ,
.Xr kvm-clone 8 ,
.Xr kvm-golden 8
.Sh HISTORY
The Btrfs subvolume handling dates from 2026-09-03, when a Fedora Cloud
golden shipped with its machine-id and host keys because the top of its
filesystem had no
.Pa /etc .
The mesh keys were added to the list on 2026-09-23, after six Kubernetes
nodes cloned with one WireGuard key overwrote one another as peers.
.Pp
On 2026-09-29, writing this page: an image file could never be sealed,
since the loop device was attached and then set aside; and the in-use
checks compared the path as typed, so a running machine's disk given as
.Pa /dev/zd192
was mounted on the host and sealed while the machine ran, and the host's
own pool partition passed every check.
The first was reproduced with the version before the fix; the second was
found by reading it.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
A disk whose root filesystem is on LVM, or whose system spans partitions
other than Btrfs subvolumes, is not understood.
.Pp
The seal cannot be undone; take a snapshot first, or seal a copy with
.Xr kexport 8 .
.Pp
The ZFS-on-root shape was not exercised for this page.
